Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Thermal Shock at Carriage 4
- Story & Context: The genre’s opening shot happened when Lucian Vane dropped his pocket groovebox onto the iron floorplates of the Charleroi night train, accidentally shorting out the carriage lighting just as the emergency brakes locked up. Through the acrid reek of scorched brake shoes and spilled chicory coffee, the rail conductor pulled out his own battery synth instead of writing a citation, jamming with Elodie's breathless flute trills while the wheels slid against iced tracks. Passengers slammed their heavy boots against the wooden luggage bins in time with the 110-BPM dembow clatter, until Lucian leaned into an old dispatch mic to bark: "Hold the blowpipe till the axle screams."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: 1950s documentary photography of a crowded train car plunged into amber darkness, illuminated only by sparks flying off train rails through the windows and the green LED indicators of handheld synths, frantic blurred hands playing a silver flute, thick film grain and water-streaked windows.

Track 2: "Did the Pontil Snap, Elodie?"
- Story & Context: In the drafty vestibule outside the Schaerbeek railyards, Elodie Ramos finally confessed to her bandmates why she was riding the night commuter trains instead of playing with the regional symphony. Her fingertips were numb inside fingerless wool gloves, her breath pluming white against the frost-crusted glass as she admitted she had pawned her father's 1952 gold flute headjoint to buy discarded synthesizer modules from a flea market stall. Over an overdriven TB-303 line crawling at a sludgy mambo tempo, she blew percussive, overblown key-clicks into a warped tape echo, whispering into the microphone: "I traded golden scales for iron teeth."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Film noir cinematic close-up of a soot-streaked young woman’s face against cold textured glass, reflection distorted by cracks, a vintage silver flute catching amber emergency light, deep shadows, moody 1950s cinematic framing with brutalist concrete platform visible outside.

Track 3: Stained Light Scratches the Iron
- Story & Context: By the time the train crested the Marchienne-au-Pont viaduct at sunrise, Darek Szyszko had daisy-chained twenty-four commuter pocket synthesizers together through the train’s obsolete vacuum-tube passenger address system. Decades of furnace soot on the carriage windows fractured the dawn into mottled red and amber shards, illuminating tired factory workers dancing on floorboards carpeted in broken bottle glass and spent dry-cell batteries. The carriage PA speaker cones finally melted under the acid bass pressure, but the heavy dembow resonance had vibrated into the train's structural steel, cementing the local myth that the bridge forever repeats: "The furnace sleeps, but the carriage remembers."
